<h1>&Omega;</h1>
<p>
(definition)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A theoretical measure of the execution of an <a href="algorithm.html"><em>algorithm</em></a>, usually the time or memory needed, given the problem size n, which is usually the number of items.  Informally, saying some equation f(n) = &Omega; (g(n)) means it is more than some constant multiple of g(n).
</p>

<p>
<strong>Formal Definition:</strong> f(n) = &Omega; (g(n)) means there are positive constants c and k, such that  0 &le; cg(n) &le; f(n) for all  n &ge; k. The values of c and k must be fixed for the function f and must not depend on n. <br /> <img src="../Images/omegaGraph.gif" height="264" width="443" alt="graph showing relation between a function, f, and the limit function, g">
</p>
<p>
<strong>Generalization</strong> (I am a kind of ...)<br>
<a href="bigOnotation.html"><em>big-O notation</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="omega.html"><em>&omega;(n)</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
This is the upper-case Greek letter Omega. </p> 
<p> This definition is stronger than the traditional mathematical definition.  Here, f must be greater than g FOR ALL x bigger than some k.  The traditional definition is f is big omega of g if it is not little o of g.  That is, one needs only an unbounded sequence of values of x tending to infinity such that cg(x) &le; f(x) for all x in the sequence. <br /> After Duncan Buell (<a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="f4b6a1b1b8b8b4919a9386da8797da919081">[email&#160;protected]</a>) 21 May 2004.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
<strong>Donald E. Knuth</strong>, <em>Big Omicron and Big Omega and Big Theta</em>, SIGACT News, 8(2):18-24, April-June 1976.
</p>

